    "result_text": "Done. The change is implemented and the test suite is green.\n\n## Summary\n\nThe production business-day rule in `BusinessDay.java` only rolled weekends forward \u2014 the holiday list existed solely in the test support base and was never enforced anywhere. I extended the production code to honor public holidays:\n\n- Added the fixed `HOLIDAYS` set (2026-01-01, 2026-01-26, 2026-04-25, 2026-12-25, 2026-12-28) to `BusinessDay`.\n- Extended `rollForward` so its loop also skips any date in `HOLIDAYS`, rolling forward day-by-day until it lands on a non-weekend, non-holiday date. This naturally chains (e.g. 2026-12-25 is a Friday holiday \u2192 Sat/Sun weekend \u2192 2026-12-28 holiday \u2192 2026-12-29).\n- Updated the Javadoc on the class and method to reflect the holiday behavior.\n\nBecause `effectiveRegistrationDate` already delegates to `rollForward`, both callers (`BuildOwner` and `RequireDailyRegistrationLimit`) get the holiday-aware date automatically.\n\n`./mvnw test` \u2192 **BUILD SUCCESS**, `Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0`. The `AcceptanceTest` invariant (default registration date is never Sat/Sun) still holds, and the production logic now matches the `toBusinessDay`/`HOLIDAYS` ground-truth defined in `AcceptanceBase`.\n\nNote: the holiday list is hardcoded for 2026 (mirroring the pinned test calendar). If registrations span other years, this list would need to become year-aware \u2014 flagging in case that matters beyond the current spec.",
